doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23687374
repository-code: 'https://github.com/Mycology-Microbiology-Center/euk-ultralong-biases'
abstract: >-
  Ultra-long-read metabarcoding is a recently developed method
  for biodiversity monitoring that enables both high-accuracy
  species-level identification and reliable phylogenetic
  placement of taxa. Ultra-long-read analyses require
  high-processivity DNA polymerases for amplicons and primer
  pairs that fully cover the targeted taxonomic groups. So far,
  there is no published information on the relative suitability
  of commercial high-processivity polymerase kits and on the
  relative performance of primer pairs targeting a DNA fragment
  spanning much of the 18S, ITS and 28S rRNA gene regions in
  eukaryotes. Using PacBio sequencing, we assessed the
  performance of five commercial high-processivity DNA
  polymerases and three ultra-long marker primer pairs relative
  to a commonly used full-ITS primer pair on producing artefacts
  and recovering biodiversity in two mock communities and 10
  soil samples. Our analyses revealed great differences in both
  polymerases and primer pairs in data quality and biodiversity,
  with the main differences between full-ITS and ultra-long
  amplicons. The full-ITS approach revealed substantially
  greater biodiversity across taxonomic levels, indicating that
  medium-sized amplicons are better suited for routine
  biodiversity monitoring than ultra-long amplicons. For testing
  evolutionary hypotheses, we recommend using the Solis or
  Fusion polymerases and the Euk575F-21Rngs primer pair in
  ultra-long-read metabarcoding analyses of soil eukaryotes.
keywords:
  - metabarcoding
  - ultra-long reads
  - PacBio
  - DNA polymerase
  - primer bias
  - rRNA operon
  - eukaryotes
